From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: thelamer <ryankuba@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 19:27:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Code the superblocks the caller marks constant as skips
 without analysis

A 64x64 superblock whose every 16x16 block the picture's constant-block map marks is the frame
before's, yet the light mode decision still refines its motion vector at sub-pixel precision,
costs its candidates, decides its partition, and transforms its residual. Where the caller also
asks for it (EbBufferHeaderType.constant_blocks_skip), such a superblock takes motion estimation's
static bypass whatever its zero-motion SAD measures, is coded by the light PD1 path as one 64x64
GLOBALMV skip through the existing bypass, with no partition pass, candidate search, transform, or
chroma complexity check, and its map travels with the library's own copy of it so the processes
behind read it after the caller's is gone. A superblock the regular path codes, a cyclic-refresh
or base-layer one under a constant rate, is left to it: that path refreshes a still picture at a
finer quantizer, which a map would hold at its old one and let the rate control's quantizer fall
until a frame coded whole came out at many times the budget.

At a constant quantizer a still 1920x1080 frame took 1.08 ms on six logical processors where it
took 1.70, and 1.13 on one where it took 2.00; at 2560x1600 1.88 against 2.86 and 2.90 against
4.78; a 256x256 change beside marked blocks 3.28 against 3.88 and 3.70 against 4.83 at 1080p.
A still frame codes to 21 bytes where it coded to 22, a frame with the change to 36220 where it
coded to 36199, and a frame of a scroll, which marks nothing, the same stream bit for bit.
